Run all RKNN clocks during NPU0/NPU1 power transitions.

The vendor driver enables every NPU clock before powering the NPU
domains and keeps them running through the transition. With only
HCLK_RKNN_ROOT and the per-core ACLK listed, the domain powers on and
the idle handshake completes, but the core block never comes up and
any access to its registers stalls the interconnect (no oops, just a
dead SoC until the watchdog fires). Add CLK_RKNN_DSU0 and the CBUF
clocks to both core domains so the pmdomain core keeps them running
across transitions.

Adapted from the RK3576 rocket bring-up patch by Jiaxing Hu
(gahingwoo/linux-rk3576-npu, patch 0007), minus the BIU reset changes
from that series, which its own history found unnecessary once the
clocks were fixed.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576.dtsi
@@ -1068,14 +1068,20 @@
 						power-domain@RK3576_PD_NPU0 {
 							reg = <RK3576_PD_NPU0>;
 							clocks = <&cru HCLK_RKNN_ROOT>,
-								 <&cru ACLK_RKNN0>;
+								 <&cru ACLK_RKNN0>,
+								 <&cru CLK_RKNN_DSU0>,
+								 <&cru ACLK_RKNN_CBUF>,
+								 <&cru HCLK_RKNN_CBUF>;
 							pm_qos = <&qos_npu_m0>;
 							#power-domain-cells = <0>;
 						};
 						power-domain@RK3576_PD_NPU1 {
 							reg = <RK3576_PD_NPU1>;
 							clocks = <&cru HCLK_RKNN_ROOT>,
-								 <&cru ACLK_RKNN1>;
+								 <&cru ACLK_RKNN1>,
+								 <&cru CLK_RKNN_DSU0>,
+								 <&cru ACLK_RKNN_CBUF>,
+								 <&cru HCLK_RKNN_CBUF>;
 							pm_qos = <&qos_npu_m1>;
 							#power-domain-cells = <0>;
 						};
